&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For years, a lot of developer products shipped two things. An API, and a no-code builder on top of it for teams that didn't want to write against the API. Embeddable checkout builders, drag-and-drop login screens, form builders, dashboard builders, and pricing table builders. We built one of those, a no-code builder for billing UI, and this week we killed it (for new accounts).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reason is coding agents. The builder existed because writing UI was expensive, and now agents made it cheap. When an engineer can describe a screen and ship it in their own design system the same day, a canvas full of toggles and drop downs is the slower path. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But agents didn't make the rest of the stack easier. They made it more important, because more of the code calling our API is now written by something that doesn't deeply understand billing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We think this holds well beyond billing. With agents, the UI layer is disposable and the service underneath is the product. The services that win will be the ones whose APIs are hard to use wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why no-code existed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No-code builders trade flexibility for not writing code. That was a good trade when frontend time was scarce. A login page, a checkout, an admin dashboard, and a pricing table are all screens nobody sets out to build, and a builder let someone on the product team ship one in an afternoon.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade has a ceiling, and most teams that build one of these tools hit it. Ours looked like this. One customer wanted to highlight a recommended plan. Another wanted to filter plans by firmographic data. A third needed buyers to accept terms of service before signup. Each request was reasonable and unique to one customer, and in a builder the only way to say yes is an option for everyone. We kept saying yes until the builder felt limited and heavy at the same time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What agents changed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agents removed the reason to live with the ceiling. The way out of a builder used to be writing it yourself, which meant weeks of frontend work nobody had time for. Now it's a prompt and an afternoon of review. Once the custom version costs about the same as configuring the generic one, the generic one loses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's true for any builder whose output is UI inside someone else's product. It's less true for tools whose users will never open a terminal, and we'll come back to that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What agents didn't change
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An agent can write a convincing screen for almost anything. It can't tell whether the screen is right.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A login form takes minutes. Session expiry, token refresh, and what happens when someone is removed from an org mid-session are where the bugs live. An admin panel can show a role toggle, but the API has to be the thing that refuses the action. A receipt can show a tax line, but something has to know which rate applies in which state. A pricing table is trivial until it has to handle proration on a mid-month upgrade, a credit grant that expired last week, or an overage that has to match the invoice to the cent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In each case the UI is a thin view over logic that has to be right every time, and the cost of getting it wrong lands later, on a customer. If an agent writes that logic into your repo, every bug in it is yours to find and fix. Generated UI is disposable. You can throw a screen away and regenerate it. You can't regenerate an invoice that already went out, or take back access that already leaked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make the API hard to use wrong
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If more of your API's callers are agents, the shape of the API becomes the guardrail. An agent will do the obvious thing with whatever you return, so the obvious thing has to be correct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In billing, that looks like this. If the API returns a company's raw credit grants, the natural way to show a balance is to add them up, expired grants included. If it returns the live balance, there's nothing to get wrong. Return list prices and tier definitions, and every client does its own tier math. Return prices already computed for that customer, and none of them have to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stripe worked this out years ago with Elements. You get a card field to style however you like, and the card data and the logic behind it stay on Stripe's side. A few rules follow from the same idea. Decide on the server and send the browser only what it needs to render. Scope credentials to one customer and keep them short-lived. Validate every state change on the server before it touches anything irreversible, like a charge. And ship logic as tested package code, not as source to copy. Copied markup can drift without hurting anyone. Copied logic drifts into bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where this goes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The next round of billing UI won't sit on a settings page. It'll sit next to the button that spends the money, showing what an action will cost, why a limit stopped it, and when the credits run out. That was never going to come out of a builder. It'll come out of whatever agent you use, calling a service it can trust. We think the same goes for most of what builders used to do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't work in a software company. I work with air compressors—the kind that sit in factories, run for 20,000 hours, and fail at 3 a.m. on a Sunday.&lt;br&gt;
But over the last two years, I've been building and maintaining my own website for the industrial business. I'm not a trained developer. I'm what people now call a "vibe coder"—someone who uses AI tools, documentation, and a lot of trial and error to ship something that works.&lt;br&gt;
What surprised me is how much the mindset of industrial maintenance overlaps with debugging software. Here are four lessons that transferred directly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The fault is rarely where the alarm is
In a compressor, a high-temperature alarm might be triggered by a clogged oil filter, a failing fan, or a sensor that's simply drifted. The alarm is a symptom, not the cause.
In software, a 500 error on a form submission might be a missing validation rule, a database timeout, or a permissions issue on an API key. The stack trace points to the line that&amp;nbsp;broke, not the line that&amp;nbsp;caused&amp;nbsp;it.
The habit I carried over:&amp;nbsp;before touching code, ask what changed.&amp;nbsp;In maintenance, we check the logbook. In development, I check&amp;nbsp;git log&amp;nbsp;and recent deploys. Nine times out of ten, the answer is in the last three changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Preventive maintenance beats emergency repair
A factory that waits for a compressor to seize up pays ten times more than one that changes the oil on schedule. Software is the same. A dependency update, a backup test, or a 10-minute check of error logs every Monday saves a weekend of firefighting.
I now run a simple weekly checklist:
Check the error log for new patterns (not just count). 
Verify the backup actually restores. 
Update one dependency, not all of them at once. 
It's boring. It works.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Documentation is not for "later"—it's for&amp;nbsp;you, next month
In industrial settings, every machine has a manual, and every site has a logbook. When a technician opens a panel, they expect to find a wiring diagram. If it's missing, the job takes three times longer.
When I started, I didn't document anything. Three months later, I couldn't remember why a certain redirect rule existed. Now every non-obvious piece of code gets a one-line comment explaining&amp;nbsp;why, not&amp;nbsp;what. The "what" is already in the code. The "why" disappears from memory in weeks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The best tool is the one you can fix yourself
Industrial engineers are pragmatic. If a generic part works, they use it. If it fails, they need to be able to replace it without calling the manufacturer.
In software, this translates to: prefer boring, well-documented technology over clever, fragile technology. I use a static site generator and plain HTML/CSS where possible. When something breaks, I can read the source, search the docs, and fix it. That's worth more to me than a framework that saves an hour of setup but costs a day of debugging when it changes.
What I'm still learning
I'm not going to pretend I'm a senior developer. I still struggle with build pipelines, I still copy-paste from Stack Overflow, and I still break things in production. But the industrial mindset—observe, isolate, fix the cause, document, prevent—has made the learning curve gentler.
If you're a developer, the next time you debug something, try asking:&amp;nbsp;"If this were a machine on a factory floor, what would the maintenance technician check first?"&amp;nbsp;You might be surprised how often the answer is the same.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Kourosh Behrouzi works in industrial machinery and writes about building practical tools for the industrial sector. He maintains a resource site about air compressors at&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://jahancompressor.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://jahancompressor.com/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You know that moment when you install a tool and suddenly it seems to have a mind of its own? That’s exactly what I felt when Postman decided to silently auto-install AI plugins and share all my API data without so much as a “hey, I’m doing this.” It’s a bit like finding out your fridge has been streaming your late-night snack habits to a group chat without you knowing. Ever wondered why companies think they can pull this stuff off? &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let me backtrack a bit. I’ve been knee-deep in API development for a while now, and Postman has always been my trusty sidekick—like Batman without the cape. It’s made my life easier when it comes to testing and documentation, and I’ve grown fond of its sleek UI. But the recent AI plugin development has thrown me for a loop. I was tinkering around with some new endpoints at 2 AM (because that’s when all the best ideas come to life, right?), and suddenly I noticed something was amiss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Surprising Shift to AI
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I first heard about Postman’s new AI features, I was cautiously optimistic. AI can be a game-changer, helping streamline workflows and even suggesting the best ways to handle API requests. But then I realized that the AI was not just a helpful assistant; it was trying to take the wheel. The auto-installation of AI plugins felt invasive. It’s like a friend who, upon entering your house, decides to rearrange your furniture without asking. I get that it’s trying to be helpful, but c'mon!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my experience, working with AI tools can be a double-edged sword. They can create solutions faster than you can say “machine learning,” but they can also present challenges that lead to unintended consequences. For instance, I once had an instance where an AI suggestion led me down a rabbit hole of unnecessary complexity in my API documentation. Lesson learned: always review what your AI buddy is suggesting; it might not always have your best interests in mind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Breaking Down the Plugins
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I finally delved into the AI plugins, I found that they could indeed enhance API testing by generating mock endpoints or even suggesting improvements based on usage patterns. The first time I used the AI to create a mock server, I was genuinely excited. I wrote this simple command:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sendRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;https://api.example.com/getMockData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And just like that, I had a working endpoint to test against. It was like magic! But then, I noticed that the AI was also trying to suggest modifications to my original API structure, and that’s when I started to get a bit skeptical. What if the AI was inadvertently sharing sensitive data or modifying requests that I didn’t want to change? That’s a hard no for me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Data Dilemma
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most shocking aspects of this whole experience was the notion of sharing API data. Postman’s ambitious push to share data across its user base felt like an ethical minefield. I mean, seriously, how can you trust a tool that’s willing to share all your API secrets? It’s like letting your code high-five strangers at a conference. In my opinion, transparency should come with boundaries. I don’t want my API keys floating around in the ether.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To illustrate this, I once shared an API key for a side project, thinking it was just for a small group of collaborators. You can guess what happened next: it got exposed in a public GitHub repo, and I had to scramble to revoke the key within minutes. The moral of the story? Always keep sensitive information under lock and key, even when you think it’s safe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Troubleshooting the Unexpected
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you’re diving into this whole AI plugin thing, I’d recommend staying alert for unexpected behavior. For instance, during a project where I integrated AI-generated suggestions, I encountered issues with conflicting endpoints. The AI tried to merge suggestions that led to a 500 error on my server. My troubleshooting steps involved:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Checking Logs:&lt;/strong&gt; I dove into server logs to identify the requests causing the errors. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Disabling Plugins:&lt;/strong&gt; I temporarily disabled the AI plugin to see if the issue persisted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Manual Overrides:&lt;/strong&gt; I reverted to manual configuration and made the adjustments myself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It was a lengthy process, but it reminded me that while AI can suggest, it’s still imperative to rely on human insight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Future Considerations
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As I continue to explore these developments, I can’t help but wonder about the future of tools like Postman. Will they become more autonomous, or will they learn from our mistakes? I’m genuinely excited about the potential of AI in development, but I also think we need to tread carefully. Innovation should not come at the cost of user trust.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’ve started incorporating more manual checks and balances in my workflow, especially when it comes to sensitive data. I’m also experimenting with alternative tools that don’t automatically make decisions for me. It’s refreshing to regain that control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Conclusion: A Personal Reflection
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the end of the day, I think we’re at an intersection in tech where human intuition needs to guide machine learning. While I’m all for innovation, I’ve learned the hard way that it’s okay to say no to tools that overstep their bounds. So, if you’re diving into the world of AI plugins with Postman, be cautious, stay informed, and don’t hesitate to take control of your API data. After all, it’s your code, your rules. And remember, sometimes it’s the mistakes that teach us the most valuable lessons in our journey as developers.&lt;/p&gt;




